    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-p/q4",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The weights of baking apples are thought to be Normally distributed. 2.5% of these apples are heavier than 250 grams and 1% are lighter than 144 grams. a) Find the mean and the standard deviation of the weights of baking apples, according to this model, giving the answers correct to the nearest integer. ( ) 6 You may use the mean and standard deviation found in part (a) to answer part (b). b) Given a baking apple weighs more than 227 grams, determine the probability it weighs more than 250 grams. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:578.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:533.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) Write X ∼N(µ, σ2). [B1] The given quantiles yield 250 = µ + Φ−1(0.975)σ, 144 = µ + Φ−1(0.01)σ. [M1] [A1] Use 250 = µ + 1.95996σ and 144 = µ −2.32635σ. Subtraction gives 106 = 4.28631σ. Subtract to find σ ≈24.7299 and substitute to get µ ≈201.5303. [M1] To the nearest gram, µ = 202, σ = 25 . [M1] [A1] (b) With the unrounded parameters, the required ratio is P(X &gt; 250) 0.025 P(X &gt; 227) = 1 −Φ((227 −µ)/σ) ≈0.1650 . [M1] [M1] [A1] The scan uses the stated tail P(X &gt; 250) = 0.025 with the whole-gram estimates in the denominator: Since (227 −202)/25 = 1, this convention gives\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"0.025/[1 −Φ(1)] ≈0.1576. Evaluating both tails with the rounded model N(202, 252) instead gives approximately 0.1729. [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:672.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The weights of baking apples are thought to be Normally distributed. 2.5% of these apples are heavier than 250 grams and 1% are lighter than 144 grams. a) Find the mean and the standard deviation of the weights of baking apples, according to this model, giving the answers correct to the nearest integer. ( ) 6 You may use the mean and standard deviation found in part (a) to answer part (b). b) Given a baking apple weighs more than 227 grams, determine the probability it weighs more than 250 grams. ( ) 5"
