    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-p/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The discrete random variable X represents the number of households with satellite TV subscriptions. It is assumed that X follows a binomial distribution ( B n ,0.35 ) . a) If n = 25 ,  find the probability ... i. ... ( P X = 12 ) . ( ) 2 ii. ... ( P X &gt; 12 ) . ( ) 2 b) If n = 25 ,  determine the probability\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q5-question-2.svg\" alt=\"  P E ( X ) − Var ( X ) &lt; X &lt; E ( X ) + Var ( X ) . ( ) 5  c) Find the smallest number of households that must be sampled so that the probability of having at least a household with satellite TV subscription is greater than 99%. ( ) 5 An analyst believes that the proportion of households with satellite TV subscription is higher, because in a sample of 25 households 13 had a satellite TV subscription. d) Using a clear method, test the analyst&#x27;s belief, at the 5% level of significance. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:577.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) For n = 25, (i) 25 ! 0.35120.6513 ≈0.06497 . P(X = 12) = [M1] [A1] 12 (ii) P(X &gt; 12) = 1 −P(X ≤12) ≈0.06044 . [M1] [A1] (b) The mean is 8.75, and the standard deviation is q 25(0.35)(0.65) ≈2.38485. [B1] [A1] Subtract and add the standard deviation to obtain 6.365 . . . &lt; X &lt; 11.135 . . .. Use P(X ≤11) −P(X ≤6). The strict interval contains the integers 7 through 11. [A1] Thus\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"P(7 ≤X ≤11) ≈0.7012 . [M1] [A1] (c) The condition is 1 −0.65n &gt; 0.99, [B1] [A1] or n &gt; log(0.01)/ log(0.65) ≈10.69. [A1] The minimum is 11 . [M1] [A1] (d) Test H0 : p = 0.35 [B1] [A1] against H1 : p &gt; 0.35. [A1] Under H0 the upper-tail probability for 13 or more is 0.06044 &gt; 0.05. [A1] Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence that the proportion exceeds 0.35 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 20 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The discrete random variable X represents the number of households with satellite TV subscriptions. It is assumed that X follows a binomial distribution ( B n ,0.35 ) . a) If n = 25 ,  find the probability ... i. ... ( P X = 12 ) . ( ) 2 ii. ... ( P X > 12 ) . ( ) 2 b) If n = 25 ,  determine the probability   P E ( X ) − Var ( X ) < X < E ( X ) + Var ( X ) . ( ) 5  c) Find the smallest number of households that must be sampled so that the probability of having at least a household with satellite TV subscription is greater than 99%. ( ) 5 An analyst believes that the proportion of households with satellite TV subscription is higher, because in a sample of 25 households 13 had a satellite TV subscription. d) Using a clear method, test the analyst's belief, at the 5% level of significance. ( ) 6"
