    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-p/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Dora, Tina and Flora are three girls which train daily in their local gym. Dora&#x27;s and Tina&#x27;s daily attendances are independent of one another, with respective probabilities of 0.9 and 0.8. Flora is Tina&#x27;s friend, which also trains in the same gym as Tina. The probability that Flora trains on any given day is 0.7 if Tina also trains on that day, but it is 0.4 if Tina does not train on that day. a) Find the probability that on a given day ... i. ( ) 2 ... all three girls train.\" style=\"--madas-width:581.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"ii. ... only two of the three girls train. ( ) 3 b) Determine the probability that on a given day... i. ... if Flora trained, then Dora and Tina also trained. ( ) 2 ii. ... if Dora and Tina trained, then Flora also trained. ( ) 2 iii. ( ) ... if Tina trained, then Dora and Flora also trained. 2 iv. ... if Tina and Flora trained, then Dora also trained. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:596.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Using a tree diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:548.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Use D, T, F for attendance: The calculation assumes that conditional on Tina’s attendance, Flora’s attendance probability does not depend on Dora’s. (a) (i) P(D ∩T ∩F) = 0.9(0.8)(0.7) . [M1] [A1] = 0.504 (ii) The three possibilities for exactly two attendees are Dora and Tina only, Dora and Flora only, and Tina and Flora only: Multiply along the corresponding branches; they contribute 0.216, 0.072, 0.056. The three disjoint cases give 0.9(0.8)(0.3) + 0.9(0.2)(0.4) + 0.1(0.8)(0.7) = 0.344 . [M1] [M1] [A1] (b) The numerator is 0.504 in each case:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The corresponding conditioning probabilities give (i) 0.504/[0.8(0.7) + 0.2(0.4)] = 0.7875 . [M1] [A1] (ii) 0.504/[0.9(0.8)] = 0.7 . [M1] [A1] (iii) 0.504/0.8 = 0.63 . [M1] [A1] (iv) 0.504/[0.8(0.7)] = 0.9 . [M1] [A1] Source clarification. The handwritten working assumes conditional independence: The calculation above uses that assumption:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 13 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Dora, Tina and Flora are three girls which train daily in their local gym. Dora's and Tina's daily attendances are independent of one another, with respective probabilities of 0.9 and 0.8. Flora is Tina's friend, which also trains in the same gym as Tina. The probability that Flora trains on any given day is 0.7 if Tina also trains on that day, but it is 0.4 if Tina does not train on that day. a) Find the probability that on a given day ... i. ( ) 2 ... all three girls train. ii. ... only two of the three girls train. ( ) 3 b) Determine the probability that on a given day... i. ... if Flora trained, then Dora and Tina also trained. ( ) 2 ii. ... if Dora and Tina trained, then Flora also trained. ( ) 2 iii. ( ) ... if Tina trained, then Dora and Flora also trained. 2 iv. ... if Tina and Flora trained, then Dora also trained. ( ) 2"
  },
