    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q14-question-1.svg\" alt=\"35° 20° A box of mass 60 kg is held in limiting equilibrium, on a fixed rough inclined plane, by a rope. The plane is at an angle of 20° to the horizontal, as shown in the figure above.\" style=\"--madas-width:558.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q14-question-2.svg\" alt=\"The rope lies in a vertical plane containing a line of greatest slope of the incline plane and is inclined to the plane at an angle 35° . The rope is modelled as a light inextensible string and the box is modelled as a particle. The coefficient of friction between the box and the plane is 1 4 . Determine the least possible tension in the rope. ( 10 )\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q14-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The smallest tension occurs when friction contributes its maximum upslope component: [B1] [A1] Put F = R/4. [M1] [A1] Resolving perpendicular and parallel to the plane gives R = 60g cos 20◦−T sin 35◦, T cos 35◦+ 1 4R = 60g sin 20◦. [M1] [A1] The greatest available upslope force increases with T, because cos 35◦−1 4 sin 35◦&gt; 0. [A1] Substitute the normal reaction into the along-slope equation, expand the quarter, and collect the two tension terms:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-p/q14-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"T(cos 35◦−1 4 sin 35◦) = 60g(sin 20◦−1 4 cos 20◦). At the lower boundary, T = 60g(sin 20◦−1 4 cos 20◦) ≈93.2 N . [M1] [A1] cos 35◦−1 4 sin 35◦ The normal reaction is positive at this value. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
